#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6c7545-300a-5f79-9ad5-7f782ed66287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,17⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330659} {:answer/id #uuid "7612117a-0b40-5aca-a74d-7b61bd70081a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-15⟩)\\cdot ⟨-1,5,0⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330660} {:answer/id #uuid "f7906bff-53ba-5d37-a117-b6295b92ec3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-21⟩)\\cdot ⟨5,0,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330661} {:answer/id #uuid "aa61c838-b3e4-5a91-adcd-a4b5442e3c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,-1,5⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330662} {:answer/id #uuid "7b76d532-b19d-53d8-af16-cf5c45f7d481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,11⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6c7545-300a-5f79-9ad5-7f782ed66287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,17⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330659} :answer-field/id #uuid "02fbfa6a-5308-5cf2-b9f7-b31ea8236a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6aa44b3e-f639-568d-9d5b-c3b1165a65d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-128383" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $5y-z+17=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,5,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-z+17 & =0 \\\\ z & =5y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=17.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,17)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,17⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,17⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2bacdd-6cb8-50d4-8afa-5d4d7bc3e608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330666} {:answer/id #uuid "65efd500-d89f-5b07-ae17-7ca8d5c2fe7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-6y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330667} {:answer/id #uuid "ff97a1c2-3ba8-50b5-9630-7c229350dc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330668} {:answer/id #uuid "b30c4d34-7509-5372-aa3a-3f0d318eaf69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2y+z-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330669} {:answer/id #uuid "12a26a86-a380-5d97-8b3e-f14defa24e02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-y-10z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff97a1c2-3ba8-50b5-9630-7c229350dc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-2y-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330668} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3f8355a-f47d-5fde-944c-7f01b71f1f6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5f496f5-82da-5347-a9dc-1d7749662c5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-330500" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,-5⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =⟨2,1,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y-10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7d4c5e8-f936-53a9-bc5f-3cca97c3b2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330673} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d0d219-ec4f-53bb-97f0-b1c5bd529520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f369a1e-a1c1-580a-b60d-5664c6315eb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675} :answer-field/id #uuid "842469ef-6b3f-52d4-b990-df6cd1e2c154" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330674} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c637def1-cd89-5a2f-83b5-1ab8c3027aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330677} :answer-field/id #uuid "c362e1f6-816b-5c65-bc37-a382d6255263" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc0bcd5d-4d1d-5e96-9f96-539ff833a355" :question/math-academy-id "q-336358" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨2,-3,-6⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,2,-8⟩.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$y\\,+\\,z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ba28ff5-ac52-516d-924d-c3aac3509bb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+y-2z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330680} {:answer/id #uuid "4d7bf099-5abd-5ebb-b7b8-f635a931e376" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+y-2z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330681} {:answer/id #uuid "5a938bfe-8aab-5fb8-b67e-43d647955b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+y+2z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330682} {:answer/id #uuid "615827cd-5437-576d-ac3c-41111e651999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2z-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330683} {:answer/id #uuid "7536e066-849c-5735-87e5-0b7eb1d522d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-y-2z-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330684}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "615827cd-5437-576d-ac3c-41111e651999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-2z-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330683} :answer-field/id #uuid "4538f7fa-876c-5ec8-825c-2a661cb26038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d501cbc7-e6c3-5ee6-bf3b-290a3eeec39d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336407" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-3,1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,0,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is\n\n$$\nn=⟨4,0,-2⟩,\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =⟨2,-3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute  $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-2⟩ & =3 \\\\ 4x-2z & =6 \\\\ 4x-2z-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "458d73e7-e69a-5e49-aa1b-7ae669f9ba1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,-1,-13⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330687} {:answer/id #uuid "fccba118-c501-5493-8815-fce15a059ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330688} {:answer/id #uuid "d13d7a66-dcff-5762-9e7c-e3f5f296895c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,13⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,-1,-13⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330689} {:answer/id #uuid "9aab0509-0713-554b-bd43-62d0da345e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,7,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330690} {:answer/id #uuid "d2448346-43c6-590b-9968-d4ed7fc8748b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2448346-43c6-590b-9968-d4ed7fc8748b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330691} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bdc4cb-6d8d-5740-a5fd-192e72ab4302" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d906b68-2682-5ae7-a3b1-5cdc64fb4dd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-70824" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $7y-z-13=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,7,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-z-13 & =0 \\\\ z & =7y-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-13.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-13⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
